00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:52.150
I've been in broadcasting my whole career.

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:55.750
I started out as an intern
at NPR in the mid nineties.

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:56.930
- And even before that Guy,

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:58.010
what about,

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:00.030
before we even get into
the professional aspect

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.490
just since we have a little
bit of time, it we'll be fun.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:03.323
Where did you grow up?

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:04.360
What kind of kid were you?

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.220
What (mumbles) sibling situation if any?

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:10.590
Give us the comic book one story.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:11.423
- Chapter one.

00:02:11.423 --> 00:02:12.410
Yeah.

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:14.850
I grew up in the San Fernando Valley

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.373
in Los Angeles, actually.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:20.780
I have two older sisters
and a younger brother.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:21.789
My dad...

00:02:21.789 --> 00:02:22.622
- You're number three or four?

00:02:22.622 --> 00:02:23.470
- Number three, four.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:27.420
My dad was an engineer
and my mom was a teacher.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.450
And at the age of 41

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:32.980
my dad decided to start his own business.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:34.920
So he started a Pearl store.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.800
He still sold pearls with my mom

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.520
that was my first sort of
taste of entrepreneurship.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.310
- How old were you when he was 41?

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:47.310
- I was, I think I was
maybe six, seven years old.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:48.583
- Okay.

00:02:48.583 --> 00:02:49.416
- Yeah

00:02:49.416 --> 00:02:50.740
- So you really kind of at some level

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:53.040
mainly remember him as an entrepreneur.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:53.873
- I do.

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:56.760
And I remember him cold calling people

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.000
and going door to door in
downtown LA, trying to sell pearls

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:03.410
at the age of 41, with
three kids at the time.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:07.610
- Do you have any sense of
what got him going on pearls?

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:08.660
- When we lived in Japan,

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:10.000
my dad was an engineer

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.610
he worked for a freight
company called Flying Tigers

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:15.320
which was then purchased by FedEx.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:16.720
And while he was in Japan

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.077
he met a guy there who was like,

00:03:19.077 --> 00:03:20.017
"Hey, you should go to LA,

00:03:20.017 --> 00:03:22.497
"I've got a Pearl company
here and you should,

00:03:22.497 --> 00:03:23.527
"I can be your supplier

00:03:23.527 --> 00:03:25.270
"and you should start
a Pearl store there."

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:26.870
So that's really how it happened.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:29.620
He had no background in pearls.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:30.580
He didn't really,

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:32.100
he spent years learning about them

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.360
and became an expert actually.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.730
He's retired today and became a gemologist

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:37.650
but that was his beginning.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.827
He started this little
Pearl store in downtown LA

00:03:39.827 --> 00:03:42.530
and that's how he sustained his family

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:44.090
for the rest of his life.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.540
- Was entrepreneurship, something that was

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:50.120
in the family from either your mother

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:53.170
or dad's side grandparents,
great grandparents for you?

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:54.003
- Yeah.

00:03:54.003 --> 00:03:57.471
I think a lot of people
with my background,

00:03:57.471 --> 00:04:00.330
my family comes from Eastern Europe

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:04.480
and many of them ended up
not surviving the Holocaust.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.000
The ones who did survive
kind of had to be reborn.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.683
I mean, they were, they
grew up at a time where,

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:12.860
they were, my grandmother was a nurse.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:14.310
My grandfather was a plumber.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:16.393
That was...

00:04:16.393 --> 00:04:18.370
- Did they, were they
born in the old country?

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:19.203
I don't know if you know this,

00:04:19.203 --> 00:04:20.270
but I was born in the Soviet Union.

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:21.580
- Yeah, I know.

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:22.413
I know.

00:04:22.413 --> 00:04:24.070
- So I always resonate with these stories

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:26.680
but I always tell my
friends and contemporaries,

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:28.490
I'm far more like your grandfather

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:30.150
than I am like you because I came

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.270
over on the boat in the
seventies, which was rare.

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:33.995
But a lot of these families
came over in twenties,

00:04:33.995 --> 00:04:35.370
thirties, forties.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.160
- My grandfather was a,

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.710
he grew up in Ukraine

00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:43.780
and he was basically

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:48.830
he was sent to prison by
the communists, by Soviets,

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:52.470
because when he was a student, he was

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:56.610
it was the book by
Theodor Herzl, was found,

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:58.700
which was called the the Jewish State

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:00.290
was found in his room.

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:01.780
My grandfather was not as Zionist.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:03.500
He didn't really know much about it.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:06.500
He wasn't interested in
it, but he was set up.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:08.090
So he went to Siberia

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:10.690
for three years actually to a prison camp.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:12.710
And when he got out, he left.

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:13.543
- Got it.

00:05:13.543 --> 00:05:16.180
- And that was sort of the beginning of...

00:05:17.276 --> 00:05:18.820
- And that was your dad's dad.

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:19.670
- That was my mom's dad.

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:20.760
- Your mom, dad, excuse me.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.010
And your dad's dad, your dad's family?

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:28.010
- Similar also from, most
Jews of European descent were

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:31.360
come from this area called
The Pale of Settlement,

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.190
which is sort of between the German empire

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:34.023
and the Russian empire.

00:05:34.023 --> 00:05:36.100
They were forced to live there actually.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:39.440
And so most Jews of European descent

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:41.590
can trace their lineage to the people...

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:42.540
- In that area.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:44.310
- And many of them were extremely poor.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:45.915
I mean, they lived in...

00:05:45.915 --> 00:05:46.748
- Shtetls.

00:05:46.748 --> 00:05:48.448
- Shtetls and the original ghettos

00:05:50.229 --> 00:05:53.480
and so they weren't,
educated cultured people.

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:54.440
I mean, they were working...

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:55.273
- Grinders.

00:05:55.273 --> 00:05:56.373
- Grinders, yes.

00:05:57.390 --> 00:06:01.770
- For that reason the modern
second, third generation

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:05.370
in the US very pushed on education.

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:08.660
Was that a big, an oh and
then your mom's a teacher.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:11.740
So are you hearing that
propaganda as a kid

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:14.410
get a good, go to a great
college, go to a great college.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:15.790
How old are you Guy?

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:16.940
- I'm 45.

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:18.107
- Right?

00:06:18.107 --> 00:06:18.940
So say I turned 45 in November.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:20.733
So same age group like that,

00:06:22.134 --> 00:06:24.930
someone who became kind
of a public entrepreneur

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:26.780
I try to tell a lot of 20 year olds.

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:28.110
You don't understand

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.770
this is the era where
entrepreneurship is cool.

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:33.390
I was actually a bad
student, a great entrepreneur

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:36.210
but every teacher and sprints parent

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:37.780
thought I was gonna be a loser.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:39.580
That's how much the brand

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:42.830
of good college dominated the seventies

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:43.663
and eighties.

00:06:43.663 --> 00:06:45.003
- And nineties.

00:06:45.003 --> 00:06:45.836
- Yes and nineties.

00:06:45.836 --> 00:06:46.784
- I agree.

00:06:46.784 --> 00:06:51.090
And I think that that brand
of prestigious colleges

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:51.923
is really waning

00:06:51.923 --> 00:06:52.950
and will continue to wane.

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:55.690
And I think about this a
lot as I save the money

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:58.500
in my kids, 529 plans, often wondering

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:00.000
what is this gonna be for?

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.850
- It's a funny conversation
Guy because in parallel

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:06.040
I also think you're
seeing the vulnerability

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:09.310
of the entrepreneurial brand
because everyone's doing it

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:10.270
but it's a talent.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:11.810
It's like everyone deciding they're gonna

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:13.290
be an MBA player.

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:14.830
It is actually very difficult to

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:18.350
be a successful sustainable entrepreneur.

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:19.570
And I actually think the brand

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:22.370
of entrepreneurship has
gotten a little bit more

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:26.100
fratty clubby, cool, or trying to be cool.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:29.390
It's actually lost a little
bit of its luster as well.

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:33.230
So now you've got these two
dynamics where both are going

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:35.250
through an interesting
transformation and it will be

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.350
interesting to see what evolves from that.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:41.130
- I really think that we need to work

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:43.090
on redefining what it means
to be an entrepreneur.

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:46.030
Actually taking it back to
what it actually really means

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:48.310
because my grandfather
was an entrepreneur.

00:07:48.310 --> 00:07:49.220
He was a plumber.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:52.100
He employed two people, right?

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:53.370
My dad was an entrepreneur.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:56.720
He had a jewelry store in
downtown LA selling pearls

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:59.933
and my mother worked with
him and he had two employees.

00:08:01.267 --> 00:08:04.150
The person who runs the
corner grocery store

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:07.320
here in Oakland, where I am,
is a successful entrepreneur

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.340
actually more profitable than Uber.

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:13.070
- Well, it's funny when,
there's a video of me

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:17.320
on the internet on an
early Bloomberg TV episode

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:20.910
of a show that was focusing
on Tech stars very early on

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:22.750
in this web 2.0 bubble.

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:25.240
And there's eight, it's
almost like a last episode

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:27.350
of real world where there's
eight companies that went

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:30.700
through the show and Fred
Wilson and all, a lot

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:32.750
of people are sitting
there with me on the panel

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:35.000
kind of recapping the season

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:36.930
and every company gets up

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:39.370
and they kind of talk
about what's happened

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:42.830
since the show and eight of
the nine companies end with

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:44.230
and we've raised $3 million,

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:47.220
and the crowd goes crazy.

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:50.247
And then finally one
company goes up and says,

00:08:50.247 --> 00:08:51.287
"Well we haven't raised any money.

00:08:51.287 --> 00:08:53.220
"We've actually been able to get clients."

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:55.430
And Guy I'm not kidding, dead silence.

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:58.500
I grabbed the mic and I
go, this is what's wrong.

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:01.900
I'm like, we're cheering, giving
up a piece of your business

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:03.000
and losing money.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:04.660
And we're staying silent

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:07.570
for people that have actually
executed entrepreneurship

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:09.453
- A thousand percent.

00:09:09.453 --> 00:09:10.940
And I talk about this in my book.

00:09:12.343 --> 00:09:16.200
I am a huge, huge
believer in bootstrapping.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:19.200
And I'm a huge believer in trying to,

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:21.200
if you are seeking out money,

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:23.220
trying to seek it out from individuals

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:26.700
and institution, family,
foundational investors,

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:29.090
not venture capitalists or private equity,

00:09:29.090 --> 00:09:30.726
because I think you're exactly right.

00:09:30.726 --> 00:09:34.430
We think that success is
raising a lot of money.

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:36.260
Now look, raising a lot
of money is really hard.

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:37.093
It's hard to do.

00:09:37.093 --> 00:09:37.970
You've got to convince people

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:39.880
that you've got a great
product and it certainly...

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:41.410
- Guy, would you agree with me

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:42.290
and I'm sorry to cut you up.

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:43.940
Would you agree with me
that the thing we maybe

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:45.980
didn't understand as
well, a half decade ago

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:49.250
that's becoming more obvious
is just how much money's

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:52.390
in the system, how much money
is actually being printed

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:54.630
where some of these
money, started coming from

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:57.510
whether it's Saudi or SoftBank and things

00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:58.640
there is a little bit more

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:00.950
of an understanding that wait a minute,

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:03.780
and then you also have the
phenomenon of Instagram, Facebook

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:07.820
and others, where you had
your corner entrepreneur

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:11.870
that was in her, his 50, 60
seventies getting excited,

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:15.760
about writing a $50,000 check
into their son's best friend

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:17.730
because they were looking
for their lottery ticket

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:19.490
as the brand new startups happened.

00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:20.323
Yes?

00:10:20.323 --> 00:10:21.650
- Yes.

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:24.143
And the reality is as you point out,

00:10:24.143 --> 00:10:26.380
I mean, there is, it's
estimated there's more

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:30.500
than a trillion dollars
of unspent cash sitting

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:31.680
in investors bank accounts

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:33.800
in the United States alone, right?

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:36.890
There is an insane amount
of money out there.

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:39.360
And what happens is, is when,

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.630
venture capital firms make a bet

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:43.617
and make a lot of money
people sort of say,

00:10:43.617 --> 00:10:45.047
"Wow, look how brilliant they were,

00:10:45.047 --> 00:10:46.940
"how much foresight they had."

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:48.760
But the reality is that,

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:50.630
they're putting their chips

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:53.080
on every single number
on that roulette table.

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:54.260
Every single number.

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:56.850
- And getting paid and
getting paid to do it.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:57.920
- And getting paid to do it.

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:59.790
- And by the way, a percentage paid

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:01.470
for everybody who doesn't know, two and 20

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.110
you're getting 2% management fee

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:06.110
which incentivizes you
to raise as much money as

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:09.012
humanly possible because now
you're making listen, Guy,

00:11:09.012 --> 00:11:11.040
this is a story I don't talk a lot about

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:12.560
after I invested in Facebook, Twitter

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.850
Tumbler made my name
in that web 2.0 sphere.

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:17.610
Before I started VaynerMedia

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:19.540
I had crazy money from billionaires

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:23.140
and family offices thinking
that I had the golden touch

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:24.580
and had an opportunity to make millions

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:27.130
of dollars a year just taking
meetings and getting 20%

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:31.050
of the upside and chose to start an agency

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:35.200
because I realized I would be
more happy being an operator

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:36.363
than an investor.

00:11:37.464 --> 00:11:40.340
- I mean I think that makes a
thousand percent sense to me.

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:41.790
I often talk about this

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:42.840
and I love that you're bringing this up

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:46.250
because I often talk about

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:48.120
about what it means to be an entrepreneur.

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:51.620
And it's not about going out

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:53.850
and becoming the next LBNB

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:54.740
or the next Uber.

00:11:54.740 --> 00:11:56.260
That's not what it's about.

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:59.410
It's about building something
that solves a problem

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:01.303
that you have and other people have,

00:12:03.117 --> 00:12:07.150
and trying to make a contribution
in some way to the world.

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:08.840
It doesn't mean you're
gonna save the world.

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:10.380
It doesn't mean that your product

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:12.550
or brand is gonna radically transform it.

00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:15.940
But if it improves something
about it in some way

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:18.370
that is more meaningful than anything

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:20.560
that almost anything I can think of.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:23.660
And so being an entrepreneur,
building something

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:26.820
creating a product or service is not

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:28.930
it's not about scale.

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:30.270
It's not about billions.

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:34.140
It's about building something
that and allows you to

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:37.960
have a sustainable,
good, interesting life.

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:40.670
- Including things that
seem out of fashion,

00:12:40.670 --> 00:12:44.050
which is the trickle-down
on your local economy,

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:48.270
aspiring your child's best
friend to do it as well

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:51.800
giving back to the community,
by donating a bench or a park

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:56.800
or even, some wine for
the local kind of PTA.

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:58.570
Let's go back to you a little bit here.

00:12:58.570 --> 00:12:59.980
So you're growing up in this environment

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:01.600
school, school, school.

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:02.970
And what kind of kid are you?

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:05.430
Are you a bookworm kid?

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:06.380
Are you a sports kid?

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:08.500
Do you have some entrepreneurial DNA?

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:10.700
How did you play out that childhood?

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:14.170
- As a kid, like a lot
of kids, I always loved

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:19.460
garage sales and washing cars for money

00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:20.293
and doing...

00:13:20.293 --> 00:13:21.520
- So you were very entrepreneurial?

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:23.050
- Doing lemonade stands.

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:23.883
Yeah.

00:13:23.883 --> 00:13:26.280
I mean, from the age of, my
first job at age 13 was working

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:28.070
at ampm Mini Market,

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:29.650
the Arco station around the corner

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:32.100
where I loved doing full service

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:34.150
because I'd get tips for filling

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:36.360
up people's cars and
washing their windows.

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:37.193
I mean, it was awesome.

00:13:37.193 --> 00:13:38.890
I always worked, and this is a, by the way

00:13:38.890 --> 00:13:40.010
this is also a different thing.

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:43.000
I think when we were
younger than it is now

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:44.870
when we were younger,
every kid I knew had a job

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:46.260
every teenager had a job

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:47.980
because that's how you made money.

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:48.813
Today it's different.

00:13:48.813 --> 00:13:51.080
I think kids do a lot of activities

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:52.440
and are kind of over-scheduled,

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:54.040
and I'm not I don't
wanna say this is better

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:54.873
or this is worse.

00:13:54.873 --> 00:13:57.930
- And by the way, you're
talking general trends

00:13:57.930 --> 00:13:59.790
obviously there's a million
families listening right

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:01.720
now that have a 15 year old working.

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:02.789
- Great.

00:14:02.789 --> 00:14:04.380
- And obviously, and by
the way, to your point

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:07.230
it also matters what
neighborhood you're growing up in

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:10.430
to your point upper-middle-class
and above scheduled

00:14:10.430 --> 00:14:12.230
on 73 different activities,

00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:15.330
lower middle-class and below
you've got a lot of people

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:16.850
working minimum wage jobs.

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:17.683
- That's right.

00:14:17.683 --> 00:14:18.970
Exactly.

00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:21.870
And as a kid, I loved that autonomy.

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:23.050
I loved being able to do that.

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:24.720
I was really into sports.

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:26.000
I loved playing sports.

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:27.320
I loved playing all sports.

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:29.110
I was pretty good at tennis,

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:30.970
but, I wouldn't say it was a bookworm.

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:32.490
I was a good student.

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:34.210
I wasn't the greatest student,

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:36.020
but I was pretty good

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:39.503
- Did you have an aspirational
college in early high school?

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:42.000
- Here's the thing, I didn't,

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.150
my parents emphasized education

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:47.280
but they weren't really
focused on prestige education.

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:48.393
- Got it.

00:14:48.393 --> 00:14:49.340
They didn't care about status.

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:51.610
They cared about you being grounded,

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:53.440
but you didn't need to go somewhere

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:55.060
so they could put a bumper sticker

00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:59.060
and they could be, cloud
chasing with that (mumbles).

00:14:59.060 --> 00:14:59.893
Got it.

00:14:59.893 --> 00:15:00.866
- Not at all.

00:15:00.866 --> 00:15:01.699
And they weren't, it was not

00:15:01.699 --> 00:15:02.950
that was not part of the
discourse in my house.

00:15:02.950 --> 00:15:04.740
They would be happy if I went to

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:06.560
any state university in California

00:15:07.425 --> 00:15:09.260
as a kid, I was also really,

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:11.780
in our family we talked
about current events.

00:15:11.780 --> 00:15:13.500
I was really interested in politics.

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:15.930
I was a really passionate about politics.

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:19.200
And so one of the cool things
that that happened to me was

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:23.660
when I was 16, I actually
went to Washington DC

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:26.850
for the school year to
be a congressional page.

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:29.440
This is a program that
doesn't exist anymore.

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:31.562
It was stopped in the early two thousands,

00:15:31.562 --> 00:15:34.700
but every year 60 high school juniors

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:35.930
would go to Washington DC

00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:37.994
and work on the floor of the
house of representatives.

00:15:37.994 --> 00:15:38.878
- That's incredible.

00:15:38.878 --> 00:15:40.620
- And I got this opportunity

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:42.990
through my congressmen to be a page.

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:44.530
And that was transformational.

00:15:44.530 --> 00:15:47.950
I, it was an amazing
experience to be there

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:51.060
as a kid and also to meet kids
from all over the country,

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:53.770
from around the country who were,

00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:56.180
from Christian, with super
Christian backgrounds

00:15:56.180 --> 00:15:57.810
and very conservative backgrounds.

00:15:57.810 --> 00:16:01.190
And, I came from a house that
had sort of mixed politics.

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:02.300
My mom was a Republican.

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:03.400
My dad was a Democrat.

00:16:03.400 --> 00:16:07.050
I was always as a kid when
I was very young, I was like

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:09.970
my mom, I sort of was really
into Republican politics.

00:16:09.970 --> 00:16:11.460
When I got into high school

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:13.410
I was really interested in the Democrats.

00:16:13.410 --> 00:16:14.243
And I loved...

00:16:14.243 --> 00:16:15.840
- Well, I mean, we're the exact same age.

00:16:17.454 --> 00:16:20.290
So it's very easy to think
about Reagan and Clinton for me.

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:21.960
And I was also very, very,

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:25.310
and I was also born in the
Soviet Union, so I'm catching

00:16:25.310 --> 00:16:28.380
in my early years, I'm
catching cold world dynamics.

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:31.410
And, I also had a lot of
interests played tennis,

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:32.243
did garage selling.

00:16:32.243 --> 00:16:34.930
So I didn't realize how similar we were

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:35.780
which is really neat.

00:16:35.780 --> 00:16:37.060
- Brothers from another mother.

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:37.893
- I love it.

00:16:37.893 --> 00:16:39.120
I love it.

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:41.010
So that makes sense to
me where you're going

00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:42.990
with your, in my family,

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:46.600
Reagan was huge because we
had made our American dream

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:47.770
during that time.

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:50.850
He gets that credit
for me, as I got older,

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:52.900
Clinton was cool and progressive

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:54.900
and socially about the things I was about.

00:16:54.900 --> 00:16:57.060
So it made sense.

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:58.400
- Yeah, exactly.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:00.650
And that was sort of

00:17:00.650 --> 00:17:01.630
my kind of life.

00:17:01.630 --> 00:17:02.950
And I went off to college

00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:07.950
and really I think my
plan was to do something

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:11.730
in public service either in
journalism or in politics.

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:14.320
I was always also really
interested in that.

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:16.280
In high school I did the student newspaper

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:18.300
and then in college, I did too.

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:21.460
So I was all, I was already kind of...

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:23.090
- What state did you go to college?

00:17:23.090 --> 00:17:24.440
- In Massachusetts,

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:26.060
- And then what?

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:28.163
- And then after college,

00:17:29.082 --> 00:17:30.610
I looked around for newspaper jobs.

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:32.230
I couldn't find, I couldn't get one,

00:17:32.230 --> 00:17:35.800
because at that time, this
is now the mid nineties.

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:37.100
- 97 graduated or 98.

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:38.070
- 96.

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:39.270
I graduated college.

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:44.100
If you were an ambitious
journalist at the time

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:45.560
you would wanna work for a newspaper.

00:17:45.560 --> 00:17:47.610
So trying to get a job at a place

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:51.130
like the Baltimore sun or
the Star Ledger in Newark

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:54.059
or the Boston Globe was impossible.

00:17:54.059 --> 00:17:58.270
They took Harvard and
Princeton and Yale graduates.

00:17:58.270 --> 00:18:00.240
I could not get a job
at any of those places.

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:03.210
So I actually stumbled into an internship

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:07.750
at NPR when I was 22 or 21, I think.

00:18:07.750 --> 00:18:10.240
And that was, I didn't grow
up in an NPR household.

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:11.530
My parents had never heard of it.

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:13.550
They didn't know what it was.

00:18:13.550 --> 00:18:15.250
They thought I was working
at a radio station.

00:18:15.250 --> 00:18:17.650
I first heard it in college.

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:18.920
I just like tuned the dial.

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:20.620
And I heard the, I heard Carr Talk.

00:18:20.620 --> 00:18:21.840
And I was like, what is this?

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:23.050
This is so crazy.

00:18:23.050 --> 00:18:23.883
This is so good.

00:18:23.883 --> 00:18:24.716
I love this.

00:18:24.716 --> 00:18:27.380
I wanna have like a potluck
dinner with these guys.

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:28.330
And that's sort of,

00:18:28.330 --> 00:18:30.360
and I discovered that they didn't actually

00:18:30.360 --> 00:18:32.230
work at NPR in Washington,

00:18:32.230 --> 00:18:35.160
but that's how I got into to journalism.

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:36.830
And that's how it happened.

00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:39.300
- And that's kind of you got

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:42.133
into in that world and stayed in it.

00:18:42.133 --> 00:18:43.360
- I got into that world

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:45.220
and that was sort of my career

00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:48.423
for the next, sort of 15 years.

00:18:50.287 --> 00:18:52.000
I started out as an intern

00:18:52.845 --> 00:18:54.400
and at night I was writing articles

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:56.670
for the Washington city
paper in Washington, DC.

00:18:56.670 --> 00:19:00.640
This was a time when alternative
weeklies really mattered.

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:01.550
- Big time.

00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:02.383
- Yes.

00:19:02.383 --> 00:19:03.900
I mean, this was like,

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:06.490
the editor of that paper
was a guy named David Carr

00:19:06.490 --> 00:19:09.620
who went on to become a
legendary journalist in his own

00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:12.920
like Jake Tapper's on CNN now as a writer

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:14.883
Ta-Nehisi Coates the great,

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:17.923
like intellectual writer was,

00:19:17.923 --> 00:19:19.790
this amazing group of people.

00:19:19.790 --> 00:19:21.690
So that's where I really
kind of cut my teeth

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:24.140
and then eventually
became a reporter for NPR.

00:19:24.140 --> 00:19:27.710
And very early in my career
had a chance to go overseas.

00:19:27.710 --> 00:19:29.050
So I spent almost six

00:19:29.050 --> 00:19:33.230
and a half years overseas covering,

00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:35.670
eventually it ended up
covering a war and conflict,

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:37.100
which wasn't my intention.

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:40.270
But, 9/11, many people

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:43.090
who were foreign correspondents
became war correspondence.

00:19:43.090 --> 00:19:43.923
- That's right.

00:19:43.923 --> 00:19:44.930
Where would you live?

00:19:45.860 --> 00:19:48.030
- I was posted in Berlin.

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:51.610
I was posted in Jerusalem
and I was posted in London

00:19:51.610 --> 00:19:54.210
but I really didn't live
in those places much.

00:19:54.210 --> 00:19:56.830
I was usually on the
road, when I was in London

00:19:56.830 --> 00:19:59.720
I was in Iraq all the time or Afghanistan

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:03.070
when I was in Jerusalem,
I was in the West Bank

00:20:03.070 --> 00:20:03.903
or in Gaza,

00:20:03.903 --> 00:20:06.053
or covering that conflict.

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:10.010
So it was a pretty amazing

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:11.820
incredible experience that I...

00:20:11.820 --> 00:20:15.000
- Was you family scared
for your wellbeing?

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:16.450
- Yes, they were.

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:17.730
They were

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:19.100
It was a different time of my life.

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:19.933
I was younger.

00:20:19.933 --> 00:20:21.230
I didn't have kids.

00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:24.103
My now wife was my then girlfriend.

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:26.393
And she,

00:20:28.625 --> 00:20:30.560
for two of those seven years overseas

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:32.540
I actually was a television correspondent.

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:33.680
I worked for CNN.

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:35.740
I was poached by CNN.

00:20:35.740 --> 00:20:39.450
And then I went back to work
for NPR for a few more years.

00:20:39.450 --> 00:20:42.870
And my wife saw me on television in Iraq,

00:20:42.870 --> 00:20:44.450
with those night vision goggles

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:46.730
there were just like tracer
fire going around all over me

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:48.610
she was freaking out.

00:20:48.610 --> 00:20:51.170
She's watching this from London on TV.

00:20:51.170 --> 00:20:54.610
But that, yeah, I mean, it
was a chapter in my life

00:20:54.610 --> 00:20:55.860
and a really important

00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:57.960
and powerful and profound part of my life.

00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:01.540
- How did this new show

00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:03.360
which is now a book four years ago,

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:04.310
how did that start?

00:21:05.650 --> 00:21:09.920
- Well, I became a news anchor at NPR

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:13.203
eventually hosted All Things
Considered on the weekend.

00:21:14.635 --> 00:21:16.585
And then I left the news side of things

00:21:17.450 --> 00:21:19.370
to start a new show
called the TED Radio Hour

00:21:19.370 --> 00:21:22.660
which was a collaboration
that I did with TED Talks.

00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:25.153
And that was just an amazing experience.

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:29.200
Meantime, in my head,
I always had this idea

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:32.810
for How I Built This and here's
where it actually came from.

00:21:32.810 --> 00:21:34.510
So I'll go back in time for a bit.

00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:37.160
In 2007,

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:39.050
I was back at NPR

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:41.720
and I really wanted to
be a host of a show.

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:43.360
I wanted to be the host of a

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:45.970
of an evening news show
or one of the new shows.

00:21:45.970 --> 00:21:48.470
And at the time, the
executives in charge of

00:21:48.470 --> 00:21:51.440
deciding who could
become a host determined

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:53.750
that I did not have the
qualities to be a host

00:21:53.750 --> 00:21:56.160
that I didn't have the
personality to be an NPR host,

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:59.650
that I was a war correspondent
and that it wasn't

00:21:59.650 --> 00:22:01.230
I didn't have the talent for it.

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:02.660
And it was crushing,

00:22:02.660 --> 00:22:05.610
and at that time I began to think

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:07.590
that maybe this wasn't the
right profession for me

00:22:07.590 --> 00:22:10.500
that I needed to find something
new to do with my life.

00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:13.730
So I really kind of flailed
and searched around.

00:22:13.730 --> 00:22:15.570
And I very luckily landed

00:22:15.570 --> 00:22:17.320
on an opportunity to do a fellowship.

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:18.770
I applied for a fellowship.

00:22:18.770 --> 00:22:20.080
- I apologize for a second.

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:21.229
- Sure.

00:22:21.229 --> 00:22:22.062
- Did you understand

00:22:22.062 --> 00:22:23.920
that that was literally
a subjective opinion

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:26.117
of a couple of human
beings and quantify that?

00:22:26.117 --> 00:22:26.950
- No.

00:22:26.950 --> 00:22:27.783
- No, right?

00:22:27.783 --> 00:22:28.616
- Not at the time.

00:22:28.616 --> 00:22:29.449
- This is why I wanted
to jump in right now

00:22:29.449 --> 00:22:31.480
'cause I know somebody's
listening right now.

00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:33.430
And I think you can really
help somebody right now

00:22:33.430 --> 00:22:36.060
because I think what has absolutely worked

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:40.750
for me in happiness has been
when these things happen

00:22:40.750 --> 00:22:44.560
that doesn't mean the universe took a poll

00:22:44.560 --> 00:22:48.500
and said 80% of it was
the subjective opinion.

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:50.990
And by the way, a lot of
times when you're dynamic

00:22:50.990 --> 00:22:53.970
or have some skills, you're actually scary

00:22:53.970 --> 00:22:57.650
to the establishment, which
leads to them not seeing it.

00:22:57.650 --> 00:22:59.160
- I think that's right.

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:01.560
And I appreciate that you
paused to ask about this

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:02.393
'cause you're right.

00:23:02.393 --> 00:23:03.330
It is important.

00:23:03.330 --> 00:23:06.260
It took me a long time in my life to come

00:23:06.260 --> 00:23:07.210
to that realization.

00:23:07.210 --> 00:23:09.980
Now in my mid forties, it's easier for me

00:23:09.980 --> 00:23:12.000
to see it that way.

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:16.420
But, when I was in my
early thirties, 30, 31,

00:23:16.420 --> 00:23:17.790
it was very hard to see it that way

00:23:17.790 --> 00:23:21.570
a respected executive with
experience telling you

00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:23.140
you don't have what it takes.

00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:23.973
It means something.

00:23:23.973 --> 00:23:25.093
And you believe that.

00:23:26.747 --> 00:23:27.640
And I wish I could go back

00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:30.727
and tell my 30 something,
30, 31 year old self,

00:23:30.727 --> 00:23:31.560
"Hey it's fine.

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:33.696
"You got this, keep on it."

00:23:33.696 --> 00:23:36.433
In many ways I needed that
kick in the butt though.

00:23:37.347 --> 00:23:38.750
- Did you get rejected
from any of the colleges

00:23:38.750 --> 00:23:39.920
that you applied to?

00:23:39.920 --> 00:23:41.670
- I got rejected from many colleges

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:43.830
and I got rejected from many jobs

00:23:44.700 --> 00:23:46.000
and over the course of my career

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:47.763
I wish I can talk about in depth.

00:23:48.620 --> 00:23:50.080
- Do you feel like that was easier

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:51.190
'cause that was systematic

00:23:51.190 --> 00:23:52.270
and you understood that

00:23:52.270 --> 00:23:55.040
whereas this one was a little bit more

00:23:55.040 --> 00:23:57.073
you thought that person really knew?

00:23:58.840 --> 00:23:59.673
- Yeah.

00:23:59.673 --> 00:24:01.340
And it happened a few years later.

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:03.273
It happened a few years later.

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:08.700
In 2011 my dream was to be the main host

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:11.200
and anchor of All Things
Considered during the week.

00:24:11.200 --> 00:24:12.530
And I wasn't picked for that.

00:24:12.530 --> 00:24:15.290
They decided that I wasn't
the right person for that job.

00:24:15.290 --> 00:24:16.320
And I was crushed,

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:18.450
I thought this is kind
of the end of my career.

00:24:18.450 --> 00:24:20.393
That was only in 2011.

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:24.760
But the thing is that
those two pivotal moments

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:27.890
forced me to kind of reimagine

00:24:27.890 --> 00:24:30.520
and take, try to take
control over my career.

00:24:30.520 --> 00:24:33.180
So in 2007, when I was told

00:24:33.180 --> 00:24:37.070
I wasn't really capable
of being a host of a show

00:24:37.070 --> 00:24:39.370
I looked around to figure
out how to transition.

00:24:39.370 --> 00:24:41.550
I found this fellowship for journalists.

00:24:41.550 --> 00:24:42.700
It was called the Neiman fellowship.

00:24:42.700 --> 00:24:46.410
I did it for a year and that
year was transformational.

00:24:46.410 --> 00:24:50.010
I got to spend a year at
Harvard, which is all paid for

00:24:50.010 --> 00:24:52.110
I get to take classes at
Harvard business school.

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:54.110
And it was during that year actually

00:24:54.110 --> 00:24:56.880
where I took a class
at the business school

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:59.730
and was blown away because
do you know the way I thought

00:24:59.730 --> 00:25:02.300
that business school was
like charts and graphs

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:05.050
and abstract ideas, the way
they teach business school

00:25:05.050 --> 00:25:06.290
is through the case study method,

00:25:06.290 --> 00:25:07.720
I'd never heard of it before.

00:25:07.720 --> 00:25:09.470
It's basically stories.

00:25:09.470 --> 00:25:11.260
And I was blown away.

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:13.060
The first day I went to a class

00:25:13.060 --> 00:25:14.310
we were handed this case study

00:25:14.310 --> 00:25:16.000
about Howard Schultz and Starbucks.

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:18.600
And I thought, Oh my God, this
is, these are hero's journey.

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:21.010
These are stories that can be told.

00:25:21.010 --> 00:25:23.940
And I kind of filed that in my mind

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:26.290
to kind of return to later on.

00:25:26.290 --> 00:25:30.220
And then 2011, what happened
when I didn't get that job

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:32.610
I wanted, it really forced me to rethink

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:33.720
what I wanted to do with my career.

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:36.560
And at that time I began
to collaborate with TED

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:38.640
as the TED Talks people to start the show

00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:40.520
TED Radio Hour and a podcast.

00:25:40.520 --> 00:25:41.650
And by the way, at the time I was

00:25:41.650 --> 00:25:45.130
on the radio and all my
colleagues on the radio were like

00:25:45.130 --> 00:25:48.090
you're leaving the radio to do a what

00:25:48.090 --> 00:25:50.258
a podcast who listens to those things.

00:25:50.258 --> 00:25:52.570
- It was no different than
people leaving newspaper

00:25:52.570 --> 00:25:53.850
to go to blogs.

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:54.683
- That's right.

00:25:55.810 --> 00:25:57.340
- Or executives in Hollywood.

00:25:57.340 --> 00:25:59.930
I had friends, I encouraged
friends, five, six, seven

00:25:59.930 --> 00:26:04.510
eight years ago in Hollywood
land to go to Netflix.

00:26:04.510 --> 00:26:08.180
And many of them did not because they

00:26:08.180 --> 00:26:10.753
because of the perceived step back.

00:26:11.588 --> 00:26:13.790
That we learn the lesson
that if you're thoughtful

00:26:13.790 --> 00:26:16.490
and you can see the tea
leaves often the step back

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:18.290
is the three steps forward.

00:26:18.290 --> 00:26:19.123
- That's right.

00:26:19.123 --> 00:26:20.620
And I was nervous too.

00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:22.800
I was like, maybe I am crazy

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:27.000
leaving, this is 2012, leaving radio.

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:30.820
- Adversity had forced
you into a framework

00:26:30.820 --> 00:26:33.980
that allowed you to be a
little bit more comfortable

00:26:33.980 --> 00:26:34.813
with it.

00:26:34.813 --> 00:26:38.460
Those micro losses puts you in the mindset

00:26:38.460 --> 00:26:41.540
and the context to make that move.

00:26:41.540 --> 00:26:43.810
I had to, I didn't have, I had nothing.

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:44.970
I had no other choice really.

00:26:44.970 --> 00:26:47.980
I didn't, there was no
horizon for me in the

00:26:47.980 --> 00:26:49.910
using the traditional path.

00:26:49.910 --> 00:26:51.440
So I started this podcast

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:54.390
and it really blew up.

00:26:54.390 --> 00:26:55.960
We kind of rode a wave of the

00:26:55.960 --> 00:26:58.460
kind of the podcast, Boom.

00:26:58.460 --> 00:27:01.200
And that's really what led me into,

00:27:01.200 --> 00:27:04.450
a completely different
phase of my career where,

00:27:04.450 --> 00:27:06.570
I eventually started a production company

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:08.390
and then I started
another production company

00:27:08.390 --> 00:27:10.060
and I made a kids' show

00:27:10.060 --> 00:27:11.790
we we've got a kid's production company.

00:27:11.790 --> 00:27:13.563
And then, we do How I Built This.

00:27:14.650 --> 00:27:17.600
And so all those failures along the road

00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:19.800
were actually meant to happen.

00:27:19.800 --> 00:27:21.810
They were meant they were,

00:27:21.810 --> 00:27:22.793
I'm not a believer,

00:27:24.140 --> 00:27:25.640
I respect people who believe, but I'm not

00:27:25.640 --> 00:27:28.920
but I do some part of me is
like, some things are just

00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:29.753
meant to happen.

00:27:31.087 --> 00:27:33.020
And those setbacks,
which were really hard.

00:27:33.020 --> 00:27:35.080
I remember a lot of sleepless nights

00:27:35.080 --> 00:27:38.304
and a lot of just feeling
really bad about myself.

00:27:38.304 --> 00:27:40.570
- (mumbles) Meant to happen,

00:27:40.570 --> 00:27:43.680
or do you think they,

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:45.580
well, there's a way to think about it.

00:27:47.330 --> 00:27:48.480
When you just said that I'm like

00:27:48.480 --> 00:27:49.350
they're meant to happen

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:51.340
to every single person
that's listening right now.

00:27:51.340 --> 00:27:54.110
And every single person
that lives on earth

00:27:54.110 --> 00:27:57.810
and one's perspective and
what they do with that

00:27:57.810 --> 00:28:01.520
the serendipity of their DNA
of optimism versus pessimism

00:28:01.520 --> 00:28:03.440
the nurturing and nurturing environment

00:28:03.440 --> 00:28:07.090
they live in, parent
dynamics, the neighborhood,

00:28:07.090 --> 00:28:08.943
they go into this place where,

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:14.560
it's amazing to me what I think about

00:28:14.560 --> 00:28:16.950
like how romantic I am about adversity.

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:19.410
I would actually argue that
the biggest challenge I have

00:28:19.410 --> 00:28:22.530
at 45 right now is do I make it harder

00:28:22.530 --> 00:28:25.400
on myself in professional success?

00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:27.890
'Cause I actually enjoy it so much.

00:28:27.890 --> 00:28:31.210
The actual adversity, the
slowing down of the inevitable

00:28:31.210 --> 00:28:33.330
the process so much.

00:28:33.330 --> 00:28:35.060
Do you feel like they were meant to happen

00:28:35.060 --> 00:28:37.970
or do you feel like they
inevitably happen everywhere?

00:28:37.970 --> 00:28:40.730
And the framework one is in mentally

00:28:40.730 --> 00:28:42.510
enables them to see the positive

00:28:42.510 --> 00:28:44.690
or to start to dwell

00:28:44.690 --> 00:28:46.560
and think about systematic issues

00:28:46.560 --> 00:28:47.700
or blaming parents

00:28:47.700 --> 00:28:51.230
like the game of dwelling
versus opportunities.

00:28:51.230 --> 00:28:55.290
- I think that it's very
natural for humans to dwell.

00:28:55.290 --> 00:28:58.440
I think instinctively most
of us when we're faced

00:28:58.440 --> 00:29:01.460
with adversity at certain
points in our life,

00:29:01.460 --> 00:29:05.210
it's natural for us to
feel really bad about it

00:29:05.210 --> 00:29:07.250
and to try and explain it.

00:29:07.250 --> 00:29:09.680
And I think over time

00:29:09.680 --> 00:29:12.900
the way you explain those things
to yourself changes, right?

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:16.920
And so now, and really
the only way to figure

00:29:16.920 --> 00:29:20.360
out how to confront adversity
is to experience it again

00:29:20.360 --> 00:29:21.610
and again and again, right?

00:29:21.610 --> 00:29:23.410
It's like any kind of muscle

00:29:23.410 --> 00:29:25.560
you just get better at it over time.

00:29:25.560 --> 00:29:26.510
- I'm sorry to interrupt

00:29:26.510 --> 00:29:28.130
because I'm passionate about this.

00:29:28.130 --> 00:29:31.853
One of my great fears was
the demonization of losing.

00:29:32.860 --> 00:29:35.620
That parents modern parenting, again

00:29:35.620 --> 00:29:39.560
generalizing modern parenting
has created we've demonized

00:29:39.560 --> 00:29:42.960
losing, kids watch parents
fight coaches and parents

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:45.870
excuse me and teachers
on behalf of their kids

00:29:45.870 --> 00:29:47.750
when they're faced with adversity,

00:29:47.750 --> 00:29:50.720
we've demonized that so
much in the last chapter

00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:51.860
still kind of now, though

00:29:51.860 --> 00:29:53.790
there is an undercurrent
of changing it a little bit

00:29:53.790 --> 00:29:58.076
but I do feel a lot of cliche, 20,

00:29:58.076 --> 00:30:02.640
18 to 25 right now struggle
because they were put

00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:06.870
in a cocoon that really demonize losing

00:30:06.870 --> 00:30:09.360
in a world where repetition

00:30:09.360 --> 00:30:12.033
of it really does lead
to happiness, thoughts.

00:30:13.390 --> 00:30:16.980
- Gary, I think I have a theory about this

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:21.060
so I'm sure you've come across
a lot of 18 to 25 year olds

00:30:21.060 --> 00:30:26.020
or maybe 18 to 30 year olds
who suffer from anxiety

00:30:26.020 --> 00:30:27.510
and depression, right?

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:28.793
For a variety of reasons.

00:30:29.690 --> 00:30:33.230
And the truth is, is that I did too.

00:30:33.230 --> 00:30:37.660
When I was in my twenties,
I experienced really

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:41.500
debilitating depression,
something that I'd never

00:30:41.500 --> 00:30:43.440
experienced ever before in my life.

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:46.270
And by the way, I was on
the fast track to a quote

00:30:46.270 --> 00:30:49.610
unquote successful career
as a young reporter at NPR.

00:30:49.610 --> 00:30:53.860
But I had so much anxiety
and fear about the future

00:30:53.860 --> 00:30:56.440
and about whether I was going to make it.

00:30:56.440 --> 00:30:59.240
And I couldn't talk
about it with my parents

00:30:59.240 --> 00:31:01.130
because I didn't come
from that kind of culture.

00:31:01.130 --> 00:31:02.790
When my parents talked about depression

00:31:02.790 --> 00:31:06.100
they thought mental health
was for crazy people, right?

00:31:06.100 --> 00:31:08.920
Which was a normal way to
think about it in the eighties

00:31:08.920 --> 00:31:09.753
and nineties.

00:31:09.753 --> 00:31:10.630
I don't wanna blame them

00:31:10.630 --> 00:31:12.960
but that's how people thought about it.

00:31:12.960 --> 00:31:16.520
And I've realized with time...

00:31:16.520 --> 00:31:19.010
- You believe this is a stigma issue

00:31:19.010 --> 00:31:21.970
and much like other things
we've talked about in society.

00:31:21.970 --> 00:31:23.630
This has been a continuum.

00:31:23.630 --> 00:31:25.130
and just thank God we're

00:31:25.130 --> 00:31:27.890
in a place now where we can
talk about it more openly.

00:31:27.890 --> 00:31:28.860
- And here's what I think.

00:31:28.860 --> 00:31:31.260
I don't necessarily think
young people are any different

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:32.610
than we were Gen Xers were.

00:31:32.610 --> 00:31:33.604
- I agree with you.

00:31:33.604 --> 00:31:34.437
- I don't think they're any different.

00:31:34.437 --> 00:31:35.270
Actually I think they're better.

00:31:35.270 --> 00:31:36.720
I think they're more inspiring.

00:31:36.720 --> 00:31:39.100
I think they're more, they
wanna take on the world

00:31:39.100 --> 00:31:40.010
in a more inspiring way.

00:31:40.010 --> 00:31:42.420
- I think they're more
inspiring and inclusive

00:31:42.420 --> 00:31:44.530
and open and woke and all those things.

00:31:44.530 --> 00:31:47.550
I do think that losing was demonized

00:31:47.550 --> 00:31:49.890
for them in a way that it wasn't for us.

00:31:49.890 --> 00:31:51.550
- I think that part may be true

00:31:51.550 --> 00:31:53.220
but I think the fundamentals are the same,

00:31:53.220 --> 00:31:55.810
which is think about it your whole life...

00:31:55.810 --> 00:31:57.530
For most young people, there's a track.

00:31:57.530 --> 00:31:59.330
You go from first grade to second grade

00:31:59.330 --> 00:32:01.340
and then you go to middle
school and then high school.

00:32:01.340 --> 00:32:02.870
And then if you're
lucky, you go to college

00:32:02.870 --> 00:32:04.160
and people are cheering you on.

00:32:04.160 --> 00:32:05.080
They're saying you got this

00:32:05.080 --> 00:32:06.600
and you know what's coming up next.

00:32:06.600 --> 00:32:08.010
And then a lot of people go to college

00:32:08.010 --> 00:32:09.790
and they develop their identity there.

00:32:09.790 --> 00:32:11.440
They might get involved in sports

00:32:13.941 --> 00:32:14.870
or they might organize a rally

00:32:14.870 --> 00:32:15.760
or a concert

00:32:15.760 --> 00:32:16.690
or they're in the newspaper

00:32:16.690 --> 00:32:17.600
and they have an identity

00:32:17.600 --> 00:32:18.920
and then they graduate

00:32:18.920 --> 00:32:19.753
and everyone's like,

00:32:19.753 --> 00:32:20.690
"Yes, you got this, you got this."

00:32:20.690 --> 00:32:22.790
And then they start life.

00:32:22.790 --> 00:32:23.950
And the safety net's gone

00:32:23.950 --> 00:32:26.110
they don't know what's coming next year.

00:32:26.110 --> 00:32:27.000
Add to that.

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:29.970
All of this research we now
have about the human brain.

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:33.200
We know the human brain
continues to develop

00:32:33.200 --> 00:32:34.240
that your executive

00:32:34.240 --> 00:32:37.860
your prefrontal cortex continues
to develop until age 30.

00:32:37.860 --> 00:32:40.680
So there's a slosh of
things going on in your head

00:32:40.680 --> 00:32:43.000
at the same time, you're
starting life out.

00:32:43.000 --> 00:32:45.250
There's no safety net
and you don't know what

00:32:46.278 --> 00:32:47.741
you're not going to grade 13.

00:32:47.741 --> 00:32:49.600
You're not going to the
next year of college.

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:53.320
So it makes a lot of sense
to me that young people,

00:32:53.320 --> 00:32:55.570
people in their twenties are

00:32:55.570 --> 00:33:00.360
they're so susceptible to
being anxious and depressed

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:02.590
because of all these things happening.

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:05.180
And I think really to me

00:33:05.180 --> 00:33:07.000
the problem is we don't
talk about it enough.

00:33:07.000 --> 00:33:09.363
